WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:02.750
MARK MANDEL: Hi and
welcome to Managing

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:04.370
Multiplayer Game Servers.

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.680
My name is Mark Mandel, and
I'm a developer advocate

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:08.080
for Google Cloud for games.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:10.970
Today I want to talk about
large-scale, multiplayer,

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:13.850
online games and how Google
Cloud's products can support

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:15.860
these types of workloads
for a player base

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.353
that spans the world.

00:00:17.353 --> 00:00:18.770
At the foundational
level, we have

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:22.370
Kubernetes and Google
Kubernetes Engine, or GKE.

00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:25.190
The combination of having
both a managed platform of GKE

00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:27.297
as well as the open-source
nature of Kubernetes

00:00:27.297 --> 00:00:29.630
means that we can place our
game infrastructure anywhere

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:31.410
in the world that
players show up,

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:33.770
ensuring a low-latency,
real-time experience

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:35.000
for everyone.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.112
But Kubernetes is
just the beginning.

00:00:37.112 --> 00:00:38.570
Another open source
project you may

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:41.500
be familiar with from
Google Cloud is Agones.

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.610
Agones is designed as
a batteries-included,

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.670
open-source, dedicated game
server hosting and scaling

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:49.220
project built on
top of Kubernetes,

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:51.410
with the flexibility
you need to tailor it

00:00:51.410 --> 00:00:53.600
to the needs of your
multiplayer game.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.000
Since going 1.0
in September 2019,

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.120
Agones continues to grow its
suite of tools specifically

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:01.640
tailored to running
multiplayer game servers on top

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:03.232
of Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:03.232 --> 00:01:04.940
The relatively new
offering in this suite

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:06.710
of multiplayer
game hosting tools

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:09.050
is Google Cloud Game Servers,
which is in public beta

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:10.160
right now.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:11.900
Agones is a fantastic
project that

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:13.700
excels at writing
dedicated game servers

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.740
within a single
Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:17.540
Cloud Game Servers
is a management layer

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:19.130
that sits on top
of Agones to power

00:01:19.130 --> 00:01:22.410
your global, multi-clustered
game server workloads.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:25.370
It provides you with the ability
to connect Kubernetes clusters

00:01:25.370 --> 00:01:28.190
from Google Cloud as well
as hybrid and multi-cloud,

00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:30.680
not locking you into a
single cloud provider.

00:01:30.680 --> 00:01:33.255
It also gives you a high degree
of flexibility and control

00:01:33.255 --> 00:01:35.630
since you still have access
to your underlying Kubernetes

00:01:35.630 --> 00:01:36.680
clusters.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:38.330
On top of that,
powerful features

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:39.957
provide visibility
and simplicity

00:01:39.957 --> 00:01:42.290
in running, scaling, and
orchestrating your game servers

00:01:42.290 --> 00:01:44.030
for your global player base.

00:01:44.030 --> 00:01:47.330
Cloud Game Servers has
several concepts at its core.

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:49.160
First, a Game Server
cluster, which

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:50.810
is a Kubernetes
cluster that has Agones

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:54.170
installed and is registered
with Cloud Game Servers.

00:01:54.170 --> 00:01:57.080
Secondly, you also need a way
to logically group together

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.790
your Game Server
clusters in a way

00:01:58.790 --> 00:02:01.880
that makes sense for the latency
requirements for your game.

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:03.500
We call this concept realms.

00:02:03.500 --> 00:02:05.420
And it gives you the
ability to group clusters

00:02:05.420 --> 00:02:07.700
as you see fit for
retrieving ready Game

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.639
Servers from across a realm
via multi-cluster game server

00:02:10.639 --> 00:02:11.940
allocation.

00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:14.060
It can also be used
as a target for all

00:02:14.060 --> 00:02:16.880
or some of your Game Server
fleet deployments and updates.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:20.210
Finally, we have Game Server
configurations and deployments.

00:02:20.210 --> 00:02:22.220
These are Agones-based
configurations

00:02:22.220 --> 00:02:24.650
that are able to be
deployed across some or all

00:02:24.650 --> 00:02:26.480
of your clusters
around the globe,

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:28.550
defining both your fleets
of ready Game Servers

00:02:28.550 --> 00:02:30.830
as well as autoscaling
capabilities.

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:33.500
For example, if we had
a project called Game X,

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:35.660
we could split it across
three separate realms,

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:37.700
such as US, Europe, and Japan.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:40.540
Within each realm, we can have
a series of Game Server clusters

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:43.670
hosted on Google Cloud, On-prem,
or potentially other clouds

00:02:43.670 --> 00:02:45.000
as well.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:46.550
Finally, we would
roll out our fleets

00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:48.530
of ready Game Servers
across our realms

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:51.530
using Game Server deployments
and configurations.

00:02:51.530 --> 00:02:53.390
Getting started with
Cloud Game Servers

00:02:53.390 --> 00:02:54.860
is relatively straightforward.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:57.890
First, install Agones on
your Kubernetes clusters

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:00.710
and register them with
Google Cloud Game Servers.

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:03.530
Second, decide on how you want
to organize your Game Server

00:03:03.530 --> 00:03:05.000
clusters into realms.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.700
Finally, write and roll out
your Game Server configurations

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.410
and deployments
to host and scale

00:03:09.410 --> 00:03:11.510
your Game Servers across
all your Agones clusters

00:03:11.510 --> 00:03:13.290
around the world.

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:15.290
Let me show you a quick
demo of how easy it

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:17.300
is to roll out Game
Server fleets across four

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:19.490
different Kubernetes clusters.

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:21.080
As you can see
here, I previously

00:03:21.080 --> 00:03:22.790
set up four Game
Server clusters,

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:24.110
split between two realms--

00:03:24.110 --> 00:03:25.540
Europe and the United States.

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:30.677
To roll out my global
fleet, I'm first

00:03:30.677 --> 00:03:32.260
going to create my
deployment and name

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:35.460
it stk for the awesome
open-source game Super Tax Cut

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.210
that we're going to deploy.

00:03:37.210 --> 00:03:38.740
Next, we will create
a configuration

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:40.600
for this deployment,
and we'll call it v1.

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:50.440
If we have a look at the
config I pasted here,

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:53.560
we can see our configuration for
our Cloud Game Servers fleet.

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:57.100
We'll create two replicas of
our Super Tax Cut game server.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.310
This may look familiar if
you've used Agones before.

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:02.650
We could also create our
autoscaling rules at this point

00:04:02.650 --> 00:04:03.610
if we so desired.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:09.310
The config and deployment
have been created,

00:04:09.310 --> 00:04:11.710
but it has not yet been rolled
out to all our clusters.

00:04:11.710 --> 00:04:13.430
So let's do that.

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:15.110
I click Manage Rollout
and then select

00:04:15.110 --> 00:04:20.008
the config I want to roll out
to all my clusters and hit Save.

00:04:20.008 --> 00:04:21.550
Let's now look at
one of the clusters

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:23.003
we've already connected to.

00:04:23.003 --> 00:04:24.670
And we can see our
Game Server fleet has

00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:27.045
been spread across the clusters,
and they're ready to go.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.390
To recap, we saw that
we had a previously

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:41.880
configured, global,
multi-cluster, Agones

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:45.327
deployment, which was already
organized into two realms.

00:04:45.327 --> 00:04:47.910
From there, we were able to use
the power of Google Cloud Game

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:49.710
Servers to roll out
our dedicated game

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:52.840
servers across our
global infrastructure.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:04:55.230
This is just the tip of
the iceberg of features

00:04:55.230 --> 00:04:57.000
for Google Cloud Game Servers.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:58.890
There is much more to explore--

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:01.590
including multi-cluster
allocation, aggregated metrics,

00:05:01.590 --> 00:05:02.410
and more.

00:05:02.410 --> 00:05:03.660
If you're interested
in getting started

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.952
with any of these projects,
including Google Cloud Game

00:05:05.952 --> 00:05:09.390
Servers, the URLs to do so
are on the screen right now.

00:05:09.390 --> 00:05:12.440
[MUSIC PLAYING]

